WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:07.040
If you're a fan of Christopher Nolan's 2014 sci-fi&nbsp;
space epic Interstellar, you'll probably want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.160
watch it more than once to make sure that you get&nbsp;
everything out of it. So let's transcend space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.479
and time once again as we discover the details&nbsp;
that are only clear after multiple viewings.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.640
The convergence point for the entire&nbsp;
decades-spanning story in Interstellar is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.920
Murphy's childhood bedroom. There's a poltergeist&nbsp;
chucking books off her bookshelf and toying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.640
with the physics of gravity and magnetism.&nbsp;
Ten-year-old Murphy, played by Mackenzie Foy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.400
doesn't appear to be afraid of the apparition.&nbsp;
Instead, she's mostly curious. Luckily, she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.440
being raised by a father with a scientific mind&nbsp;
who has no room for superstition. Cooper, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.640
by Matthew McConaughey, instructs his daughter to&nbsp;
not just label it as a "ghost" because she doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.680
know what it is. Instead, he directs her to&nbsp;
study it and come to a scientific conclusion. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.560
instincts end up being accurate all along, and her&nbsp;
first line of dialogue hints at the final outcome.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.400
The opening moments of Interstellar feature a&nbsp;
brief tumultuous moment from Cooper's days as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.160
a pilot. His aircraft spins out of control and&nbsp;
the Gs amp up before he suddenly snaps awake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.200
safely at home in his bed. Standing over him&nbsp;
is his daughter, who looks at him and says:

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:16.900
"I thought you were the ghost."

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.799
Later, by the end of the film, we find out&nbsp;
just how accurate this statement really is.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.240
Christopher Nolan is fascinated with the&nbsp;
intricacies of time, particularly the paradoxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.840
created by toying with timelines. Interstellar&nbsp;
creates scenarios where time stretches and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.520
squeezes based on its characters' galactic&nbsp;
locations. They unknowingly trigger various events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.480
that lead them to the NASA base where Cooper&nbsp;
signs on to pilot a mission to save the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.960
Coordinates to this base arrive courtesy of&nbsp;
the "ghost" in Murphy's room. The directions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.080
are given in binary form by altering gravity, so&nbsp;
billowing dust settles strategically on the floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.360
to spell out a message. The ghost ends up being&nbsp;
Cooper himself from inside the tesseract within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:05.180
the massive black hole known as Gargantua. But&nbsp;
where did this whole chain of events begin?

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.520
As we find out later, the instructions&nbsp;
for how to find the NASA base were given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.600
to Cooper by Cooper himself. However,&nbsp;
in order to get inside the tesseract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.440
and give himself these directions, he&nbsp;
needed to have already received them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.040
thus creating a paradox for which there is no&nbsp;
answer. The notion of an unanswerable question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.800
is very much something that we should&nbsp;
always expect from Christopher Nolan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.360
so be prepared for your brain to have a&nbsp;
workout whenever you watch one of his films.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.000
Once the crew of the Endurance travels&nbsp;
through the wormhole into an unknown galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.880
they receive a clearer picture from the data&nbsp;
sent out by previous explorers on all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.320
possible planets orbiting Gargantua. After some&nbsp;
deliberation, they decide to first visit a planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.120
closer to the black hole, though its proximity&nbsp;
to the massive gravity created by Gargantua&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.400
creates some problems with relativity. Time on&nbsp;
this planet runs much faster than on Earth, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.320
every hour is equivalent to seven years back home.&nbsp;
Therefore their time must be considered a precious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.280
resource that they do not have in abundance.&nbsp;
The whole excursion is incredibly stressful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.360
and with multiple viewings, that's only&nbsp;
amplified, especially once you notice the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.400
strange ticking sound playing in the background&nbsp;
the entire time they're on this first planet.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.240
It turns out that there's a reason that this&nbsp;
ominous ticking plays consistently throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:26.800
the scene. One particular cinema sleuth on Reddit&nbsp;
discovered that these ticks occur exactly 1.25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.840
seconds apart. With a little math, you can work&nbsp;
out that each tick is equivalent to one entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.120
Earth day. This knowledge adds another whole&nbsp;
layer of stress to an already tense situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.960
and multiple viewings only peel&nbsp;
back more layers to this odyssey.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.640
The most exciting part of any space adventure&nbsp;
obviously occurs once the characters are out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.920
voyaging among the stars. This quality is likely&nbsp;
to leave you feeling antsy during your first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.480
watching Interstellar. Plenty of viewers&nbsp;
are probably anxious as the characters hop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.200
into a rocket ship and launch into space, which&nbsp;
could cause many of the most fascinating elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.880
of the story to slip by unnoticed. And that's one&nbsp;
reason why multiple viewings are so rewarding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.240
as every bit of dialogue in Interstellar has&nbsp;
a purpose, since everything comes full circle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.040
For example, Cooper's talk with Murphy is&nbsp;
a particularly prophetic conversation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.801
hits harder when you watch it again.&nbsp;
Before he leaves Earth, Cooper says:

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:24.580
"Once you're a parent, you're the&nbsp;
ghost of your children's future."

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:29.120
This poetic message winds up becoming&nbsp;
reality in more ways than one. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.200
does Cooper quite literally end up being&nbsp;
the "ghost" in his daughter's bedroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.920
he also in a way becomes a figurative ghost&nbsp;
walking into the hospital room where Murphy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.840
is on her deathbed. He's the same&nbsp;
age as he was when he left Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:48.080
stepping back into his daughter's life exactly&nbsp;
as he was in her memories: a ghost in her future.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.360
The small crew of the Endurance isn't the&nbsp;
first group of brave astronauts to plunge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.680
themselves into the wormhole near Saturn.&nbsp;
A decade before their mission departed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.160
a group of 12 was sent across the universe&nbsp;
to explore the viability of these new worlds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.480
Each individual was sent to their own planet&nbsp;
to discover the possibilities. Due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.760
potential of death for these astronauts, they were&nbsp;
required to not have any attachments on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:17.360
as explained by Amelia Brand, played by Anne&nbsp;
Hathaway. While this was a strict prerequisite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.680
for the original mission, it apparently wasn't&nbsp;
completely inflexible for the follow-up.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.800
Among the Endurance crew, half of them do in fact&nbsp;
have family attachments. Cooper has two kids he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.360
desperately wants to return home to, while&nbsp;
Brand is the daughter of the lead scientist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.640
The "no attachments" stipulation of the&nbsp;
first mission was based on the logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.600
that people would operate with the whole human&nbsp;
race in mind as opposed to individual desires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.520
We're surprised that this crucial second&nbsp;
mission lacked that same strict guideline.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.120
When Dr. Mann sits up in his cryotube, it's a&nbsp;
pleasant surprise to discover that he's played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.080
by Matt Damon, which was kept under wraps&nbsp;
before Interstellar hit theaters. That's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.320
understandable decision. If we had known he was in&nbsp;
the film ahead of time, we would've been waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.920
impatiently for him to show up during the first&nbsp;
half. Damon usually plays heroic roles, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.660
this time. Although, he's not exactly a villain&nbsp;
per se, just more of a cowardly antagonist.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.520
Mann's survival instincts contrast notably with&nbsp;
the rest of the film. At its core, Interstellar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.160
is a tale of human connection. Since the first&nbsp;
crew sent into the wormhole were required to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.520
have no family attachments, Mann doesn't share the&nbsp;
same deep connections that Brand and Cooper have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.400
This leaves him purely self-serving. He attempts&nbsp;
to mask this quality, but everything he does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.520
feels illogical. He's a man operating purely&nbsp;
on fear. Mission leaders believed that a lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.320
of attachments would help the crew work with the&nbsp;
greater good in mind, but the opposite turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.360
to be true, as the only people to survive either&nbsp;
mission are those with connections back on Earth.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.920
When Cooper is trying to console Murphy&nbsp;
before his trip, he attempts to appeal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.040
her intellectual sensibilities by discussing&nbsp;
the fascinating aspects of time relativity.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:02.400
"Maybe by the time I get back, we might even be&nbsp;
the same age. You and me. What? Imagine that!"

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:08.080
But this plan backfires when Murphy realizes&nbsp;
that her dad has no idea when he'll actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.280
be returning from his voyage. She's so upset by&nbsp;
this that she doesn't even send a reply message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.840
into space for more than two decades. The&nbsp;
resulting age difference that Cooper predicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:23.520
does indeed come into play, but it turns out&nbsp;
that his estimate is off by about 60 years.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.400
Cooper ultimately does make it back to his&nbsp;
daughter, but only in time to witness the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:35.360
final moments of her life. Residents of the Cooper&nbsp;
Space Station inform him that he's 124 Earth years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:41.200
old when he returns. That means he's been gone for&nbsp;
more than 80 years, which is quite a long time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.240
a quick reunion with his daughter. That large&nbsp;
chunk of time lost between the two of them can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:52.240
make their extremely brief final interaction&nbsp;
a bit anticlimactic. It's a powerful moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:58.320
but you just might come away wishing it could've&nbsp;
lasted longer, especially after multiple viewings.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.680
It's no secret that Christopher Nolan is a big&nbsp;
fan of Sir Michael Caine, as the Cockney actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:08.800
has appeared in almost every single one of the&nbsp;
director's films. In Interstellar, he plays the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.880
lead scientist at NASA, Dr. Brand. His sage-like&nbsp;
demeanor helps convince Cooper to embark on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:20.720
voyage, and a poetic voiceover from Caine sets a&nbsp;
majestic tone as the Endurance crew leaves Earth.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:29.280
"Do not go gentle into that good night. Old&nbsp;
age should burn and rave at close of day."

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.840
Once you know the final outcome of this space&nbsp;
odyssey, you'll only have more respect for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.600
Caine after each viewing. It turns out that Dr.&nbsp;
Brand never had any intention for the successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.320
execution of "Plan A," which involved humans&nbsp;
leaving Earth for a new planet. His formula for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:50.000
harnessing the power of gravity requires a glimpse&nbsp;
at data from a singularity inside a black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.720
a requirement he considered impossible. This&nbsp;
means that all the conversations convincing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.200
Cooper to join this mission were laced with&nbsp;
a monstrous lie. Knowing this while watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.760
Interstellar more than once will make the&nbsp;
nuance of Caine's performance more obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:08.000
He dodges questions and grows distant when&nbsp;
certain topics are brought up, and it's hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.080
not to be impressed by this legendary&nbsp;
actor even more than you've already been.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.840
The main plot twist in Interstellar occurs when&nbsp;
it's revealed that Dr. Brand knew he couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.480
complete his gravity equation. The reason is&nbsp;
because he needed information from inside a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:28.400
black hole, a task that was deemed impossible due&nbsp;
to an inability to see beyond the event horizon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.840
and glimpse the singularity within.&nbsp;
Luckily for all of humanity, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.640
Cooper is willing to sacrifice himself by climbing&nbsp;
into a spacecraft and launching himself into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.640
Gargantua. Once he and his robot companion TARS&nbsp;
make it inside, he's able to gather enough data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.840
to complete the equation. But then he has to&nbsp;
figure out how to get the information back out.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.480
Since Cooper is trapped behind an endless stream&nbsp;
of bookshelves in Murphy's bedroom, the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.200
solution is to send this information to her. By&nbsp;
manipulating gravity from within the tesseract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:01.760
he's able to relay the data through Morse code.&nbsp;
We know that there's plenty of artistic license&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.800
taken with Interstellar, but this one sticks out&nbsp;
more with each viewing. We're not going to pretend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.920
to be math experts, but we feel safe assuming&nbsp;
that complex quantum data about the occurrences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:17.760
inside a black hole probably can't be easily&nbsp;
transmitted via Morse code in most circumstances.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:23.120
Cooper's journey to return to his daughter dwarfs&nbsp;
any distance traveled by any human in history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.280
Unfortunately, because of relativity, he&nbsp;
returns to find Murphy on her deathbed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.320
The nurse at the hospital informs Cooper that&nbsp;
she's been in cryosleep for two years. The large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.640
amount of family gathered around her indicates&nbsp;
that she's lived a full life without her dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:43.040
She tells him that no one should have to watch&nbsp;
their own child die, so she spurs him onward back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:48.560
into the cosmos in search of Dr. Amelia Brand.&nbsp;
But how does she know that she's still alive?

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.160
"She's out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:57.600
Setting up camp."

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:03.840
As an elderly Murphy speaks over a montage of&nbsp;
Dr. Brand settling into her new home planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.960
it's meant to wrap up the entire film and drive&nbsp;
home the idea of moving forward in our lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.960
But we're left wondering about the lack of&nbsp;
explanation at this key moment that could've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.240
easily been remedied by a single line about&nbsp;
a debrief. Although maybe that's a bit harsh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.640
Interstellar's plot does cover over 80&nbsp;
Earth years, a wormhole, a black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:26.640
and countless light-years, after all.&nbsp;
So considering the circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.600
we can find it in our hearts to overlook some&nbsp;
omitted information. It's what Cooper would do.

